+### Product-to-visual responsibility and source consumption
+
+Product authority defines semantic information priority, required visibility
+and timing, content limits, and accessibility or behavioral constraints. The
+applicable visual authority translates those relationships into typography
+roles, weight, color attention, spacing, grouping, and responsive composition,
+and the implementation owner observes the actual result. The controller or PM
+verifies the mapping against the exact Product source and keeps Product
+conformance, visual conformance, and user visual acceptance as separate
+verdicts.
+
+When an approved Product record contains visual-looking values, identify in the
+existing handoff whether each relevant value is a binding Product constraint,
+an approved visual-authority value, or a proposed/DRAFT implementation starting
+point. This is human-readable provenance, not a new schema or enum. Do not
+downgrade an exact value already approved by the current Product Definition
+merely because final visual acceptance is pending; changing that authority
+requires the applicable Product re-entry. Conversely, do not promote a visual
+proposal into Product or visual approval through implementation.
+
+Record the exact source identity and affected scope actually consumed by the
+implementation or visual work. If the result still consumes an older source,
+state the missing newer requirements and the affected surfaces rather than
+reporting the latest planning revision as implemented. A file-hash or revision
+difference alone neither invalidates nor authorizes reuse; use the applicable
+dependency audit, gaps, and affected consumers when an executable downstream
+contract exists.
+
+For material hierarchy, connect stable Product IDs and their required semantic
+roles or exposure points to the chosen visual roles and observed
+surface/state/viewport. This mapping may live in the existing acceptance or
+review section; do not require a new document, table, agent, or token system.
+
